concepts.body:
Each awk rule is `pattern { action }`. The pattern decides which records the action runs on:

- a comparison: `$3 > 100`, `$1 == "alice"`, `NF > 0`;
- a regular expression against the whole line, `/ERROR/`, or against one field: `$2 ~ /^api/`, `$2 !~ /test/`;
- combinations with `&&` (and), `||` (or), `!` (not);
- a range `/start/,/stop/`, as in sed.

If the action is left out, the default action prints the line, so `awk '$3 > 100'` works like a grep that understands columns. If the pattern is left out, the action runs for every line.

Two special patterns run outside the normal line-by-line loop: `BEGIN { ... }` runs once before any input is read (set `FS` or `OFS`, print a header) and `END { ... }` runs once after the last line (print totals). In `END`, `NR` holds the total number of records.

Variables need no declaration: they start as an empty string, which also counts as `0` when used as a number. That's why `{ sum += $2 } END { print sum }` just works.

Comparisons are numeric when both sides look like numbers, and text comparisons otherwise. Watch out with quoted constants: `$1 == "7"` compares as text, so a field `007` doesn't match.

Use `printf` for aligned output: `printf "%-10s %5.1f\n", $1, $2`.

File names may contain spaces, quotes and even line breaks; only `/` and the NUL character are forbidden. A pipeline that passes names as lines therefore breaks on unusual names. The fix is to separate names with **NUL**:

- `find . -name '*.log' -print0` writes names each ended by NUL;
- `grep -lZ` (or `--null`) does the same for matching file names; GNU `sort -z` and `sed -z` read them;
- `xargs -0` splits its input on NUL and runs a command with as many names at a time as fit.

`xargs` turns lines of input into arguments for a command. `-n 1` passes one per run, `-I{}` puts each item into the command wherever `{}` appears, and `-P 4` runs four commands at the same time. Often `find ... -exec cmd {} +` is simpler and just as safe.

The locale changes what tools do. Under a **UTF-8** locale (the usual one today, able to store every alphabet), `[a-z]` and sort order follow language rules, `.` matches a whole character even when it takes several bytes, and ignoring case handles accents. With `LC_ALL=C`:

- characters are single bytes, so behaviour is identical on every machine;
- sorting is plain byte order, which `join` and `comm` expect;
- grep and sort can be noticeably faster on large plain-English (**ASCII**) data.

Use `LC_ALL=C` for processing meant for machines, and keep the user's locale when the output is meant for people or the text isn't plain ASCII.
